{{smaller block|The practice of ''band-work'', or comradeship, the organic action of society, has so moulded the nature of man as to create in it two specially human faculties — the conscience and the intellect. {{right|''W. K. Clifford'', Lectures, II. 283.}}}}

¶
'''bandy<sup>1</sup>''' (ban′di), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''bandied'', ppr. ''bandying''. [First in Elizabethan E., also written ''bandie'', and less commonly but more reg. ''band'' (the term. ''-ie'', ''-y'' being irreg., and due perhaps to the Sp. Pg. ''bande-ar''), < F. ''bander'', bandy at tennis, refl. band together, join in a league (= Sp. Pg. ''bandear'', refl. band together, form a party or side, = It. ''bandare'', “to side or bandy” — Florio), appar. the same as ''bander'', tie with a band, < ''bande'' (= Sp. Pg. It. ''banda''), a band, side, party, E. ''band''<sup>2</sup>, mixed with ''bande'' = Sp. It. ''banda'', a band, company, troop, E. ''band''<sup>3</sup>. The senses ‘throw from side to side’ (from ''band''<sup>2</sup>) and ‘band together’ (from ''band''<sup>3</sup>) appear to meet in the sense ‘contend, strive.’] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To throw or strike to and fro, or from side to side, as a ball in play.
{{smaller block|Tennis balls ''bandied'' and struck upon us . . . by rackets from without. {{right|''Cudworth'', Intellectual System, p. 845.}}
<poem>To fly sublime
Thro’ the courts, the camps, the schools,
Is to be the ball of Time,
''Bandied'' by the hands of fools.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Vision of Sin.}}}}
'''2†.''' To toss aside; drive or send off.
{{smaller block|If the Earth had been ''bandied'' out of one Vortex into another. {{right|''Dr. H. More'', Div. Dial., i. 17. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''3.''' To toss about, as from man to man; pass from one to another, or back and forth.
{{smaller block|Let not . . . known truth . . . be ''bandied'' in disputation. {{right|''Watts''.}}}}

