'''4.''' To give and take; exchange, especially contentiously: as, to ''bandy'' compliments; to ''bandy'' words, reproaches, etc.
{{smaller block|Do you ''bandy'' looks with me, you rascal?
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>I’ll not ''bandy''
Words with your mightiness.</poem>
{{right|''Massinger'', Emperor of the East, iv. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Mischief, spirit, and glee sparkled all over her face as she thus ''bandied'' words with the old Cossack, who almost equally enjoyed the tilt. {{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xii.}}}}
'''5†.''' To discuss; debate.
{{smaller block|<poem>O, what a thing is man,
To ''bandy'' factions of distemper’d passions
Against the sacred Providence above him!</poem>
{{right|''Ford'', Lover’s Melancholy, v. 1.}}}}
'''6†.''' To band together; league: chiefly reflexive.
{{smaller block|All the kings of the earth ''bandy'' themselves to fight with him. ''Hughes'', Saints Losse (1632), p. 38. (''N. E. D.'')}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To bound, as a ball that is struck.—'''2†.''' To form a band or league.—'''3.''' To contend; strive, whether in emulation or in enmity.
{{smaller block|One fit to ''bandy'' with thy lawless sons.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., i. 2.}}}}

